From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zhugengyu <zhugengyu@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 18 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] sdio: (xmit) Sleep one tick on a pass that leaves frames
 behind

The transmit handler retries in two places: when a pass reports a missing
buffer or an open aggregation window, and when exactly one frame is still
pending afterwards.  Both retried with rtw_yield_os(), and the second path
with no wait at all.  On an RTOS port the completion and receive path that
returns the missing buffer or closes the window runs at a lower priority
than this thread, so a yield - which only rotates the threads at this
priority - never lets it run.  The handler then spins forever, starving
the receive path it is waiting for.

Sleep one tick in both retry paths.  Neither is the steady-state drain: the
first waits for the rest of the system by definition, and the second has one
frame left.

Signed-off-by: zhugengyu <zhugengyu@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 hal/rtl8188f/sdio/rtl8189fs_xmit.c | 26 +++++++++++++++-----------
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

--- a/hal/rtl8188f/sdio/rtl8189fs_xmit.c
+++ b/hal/rtl8188f/sdio/rtl8189fs_xmit.c
@@ -570,15 +570,16 @@
 		}
 		#endif
 
-		if (padapter->registrypriv.wifi_spec)
-			rtw_msleep_os(1);
-		else {
-			#ifdef CONFIG_REDUCE_TX_CPU_LOADING
-			rtw_msleep_os(1);
-			#else
-			rtw_yield_os();
-			#endif
-		}
+		/*
+		 * This pass left frames behind: whatever it waits for (a buffer, a
+		 * frame, or the end of an aggregation window) is returned by the
+		 * receive/completion path, and on this port that path runs below
+		 * this thread.  A plain yield only rotates the threads at this
+		 * priority, so the lower-priority path never runs and the wait
+		 * never ends; sleep one tick instead.  This is a retry pass, not
+		 * the steady-state drain.
+		 */
+		rtw_msleep_os(1);
 		goto next;
 	}
 
@@ -586,9 +587,13 @@
 	ret = rtw_txframes_pending(padapter);
 	_exit_critical_bh(&pxmitpriv->lock, &irql);
 	if (ret == 1) {
-#ifdef CONFIG_REDUCE_TX_CPU_LOADING
+		/*
+		 * One frame left: give the rest of the system its turn before the
+		 * next pass.  Without this the handler is a tight loop that starves
+		 * every equal- and lower-priority thread whenever the last frame
+		 * cannot go out at once.
+		 */
 		rtw_msleep_os(1);
-#endif
 		goto next;
 	}
 
